     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Since there are two statements, you need four rows for all possible combinations.</p>\n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span data-math=\"p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p \\to {\\text{ }}(q \\wedge p)\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span></p>\n<p>The negation of a statement has the opposite truth value. Write the opposite value of <span data-math=\"p\"></span> in this column.</p> \n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span data-math=\"p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p \\to {\\text{ }}(q \\wedge p)\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span></p>\n<p>A conjunction is true only when both statements are true. Otherwise, it is false. Write T if both the <span data-math=\"q\"></span> and <span data-math=\"p\"></span> columns have a T. Otherwise, write an F.</p> \n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span data-math=\"p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p \\to {\\text{ }}(q \\wedge p)\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p \\to {\\text{ }}(q \\wedge p)\"></span></p>\n<p>A conditional is only false when the premise is true, and the conclusion is false. Write F if the <span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span> column has an T and the <span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span> column has an F. Otherwise, write T.</p>\n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span data-math=\"p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"q \\wedge p\"></span></td>\n<td><span data-math=\"{\\text{~}}p \\to {\\text{ }}(q \\wedge p)\"></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>F</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The statement is not valid because the last column is not all true.</p>"
